\subsubsection{Sensitivity and degradation}
\label{sec:ou-robustness}
At $H_s=\SI{1.5}{m}$, one-factor-at-a-time (OFAT) sweeps multiply
$\tau$, $\sigma_{aw}$, or $r_S$ by $0.5$, $0.75$, $1$, $1.25$, and $1.5$,
holding the other two fixed. Two further sweeps follow the deployed
SpectralMSE coupling:
\begin{align}
 r_S(c\sigma_a)/r_S(\sigma_a)&=c^{6/7},
 \label{eq:robustness-sigma-coupling}\\
 r_S(c\tau)/r_S(\tau)&=c^{24/7}\sqrt{T_S(\tau)/T_S(c\tau)}.
 \label{eq:robustness-tau-coupling}
\end{align}
For unclamped cadence, the second ratio is $c^{41/14}$.
Figure~\ref{fig:ou_robustness_sensitivity} and
Table~\ref{tab:ou_robustness_sensitivity} report all five sweeps. Direct
OU-amplitude sensitivity is nearly flat, whereas regularizer perturbation
has a larger effect. Coupling a halved $\tau$ into the regularizer raises
normalized vertical error from $5.09$ to $12.87\%$; the corresponding OFAT
$\tau$ perturbation gives $5.36\%$. Thus sensitivity along the adaptation
law differs substantially from sensitivity to its prior parameters alone.

\input{w3d-ou-robustness-sensitivity-current-generated.tex-part}
