At $H_s=\SI{0.05}{m}$, the ten-seed vertical RMS is
\SI{\OURobustnessLowStressAbsolute}{m}, or
\OURobustnessLowStressMean\% of $H_s$ (Fig.~\ref{fig:ou_robustness_stress}).
The increased relative error exposes the sensor-error floor.
Compressing the low-to-high crossfade from \SI{120}{s} to \SI{30}{s}
changes Adaptive error from \OURobustnessControlledAdaptiveMean\% to
\OURobustnessRapidAdaptiveMean\% of final $H_s$, a paired increase of
\OURobustnessRapidDifference{} percentage points
($[\OURobustnessRapidDifferenceLow,\OURobustnessRapidDifferenceHigh]$).
Adaptive remains below FixedNominal by the signed difference
\OURobustnessRapidAdaptationDifference{} percentage points
($[\OURobustnessRapidAdaptationLow,\OURobustnessRapidAdaptationHigh]$).
Shortening the blend also changes the scoring-window composition, so this
is a degradation result, not a pure adaptation-rate measurement. The rapid one-way case
supplements the low--high--low diagnostic in
Sec.~\ref{sec:roundtrip-transition-ablation}, which separates both
transition directions and recovery intervals.
